<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>
<pre>
<i>To the Stockholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     Operating earnings in 1977 of $21,904,000, or $22.54 per=20
share, were moderately better than anticipated a year ago.  Of=20
these earnings, $1.43 per share resulted from substantial=20
realized capital gains by Blue Chip Stamps which, to the extent=20
of our proportional interest in that company, are included in our=20
operating earnings figure.  Capital gains or losses realized=20
directly by Berkshire Hathaway Inc. or its insurance subsidiaries=20
are not included in our calculation of operating earnings.  While=20
too much attention should not be paid to the figure for any=20
single year, over the longer term the record regarding aggregate=20
capital gains or losses obviously is of significance.

     Textile operations came in well below forecast, while the=20
results of the Illinois National Bank as well as the operating=20
earnings attributable to our equity interest in Blue Chip Stamps=20
were about as anticipated.  However, insurance operations, led=20
again by the truly outstanding results of Phil Liesche=92s=20
managerial group at National Indemnity Company, were even better=20
than our optimistic expectations.

     Most companies define =93record=94 earnings as a new high in=20
earnings per share.  Since businesses customarily add from year=20
to year to their equity base, we find nothing particularly=20
noteworthy in a management performance combining, say, a 10%=20
increase in equity capital and a 5% increase in earnings per=20
share.  After all, even a totally dormant savings account will=20
produce steadily rising interest earnings each year because of=20
compounding.

     Except for special cases (for example, companies with=20
unusual debt-equity ratios or those with important assets carried=20
at unrealistic balance sheet values), we believe a more=20
appropriate measure of managerial economic performance to be=20
return on equity capital.  In 1977 our operating earnings on=20
beginning equity capital amounted to 19%, slightly better than=20
last year and above both our own long-term average and that of=20
American industry in aggregate.  But, while our operating=20
earnings per share were up 37% from the year before, our=20
beginning capital was up 24%, making the gain in earnings per=20
share considerably less impressive than it might appear at first=20
glance.

     We expect difficulty in matching our 1977 rate of return=20
during the forthcoming year.  Beginning equity capital is up 23%=20
from a year ago, and we expect the trend of insurance=20
underwriting profit margins to turn down well before the end of=20
the year.  Nevertheless, we expect a reasonably good year and our=20
present estimate, subject to the usual caveats regarding the=20
frailties of forecasts, is that operating earnings will improve=20
somewhat on a per share basis during 1978.

<i>Textile Operations</i>

     The textile business again had a very poor year in 1977.  We=20
have mistakenly predicted better results in each of the last two=20
years.  This may say something about our forecasting abilities,=20
the nature of the textile industry, or both.  Despite strenuous=20
efforts, problems in marketing and manufacturing have persisted. =20
Many difficulties experienced in the marketing area are due=20
primarily to industry conditions, but some of the problems have=20
been of our own making.

     A few shareholders have questioned the wisdom of remaining=20
in the textile business which, over the longer term, is unlikely=20
to produce returns on capital comparable to those available in=20
many other businesses.  Our reasons are several: (1) Our mills in=20
both New Bedford and Manchester are among the largest employers=20
in each town, utilizing a labor force of high average age=20
possessing relatively non-transferable skills.  Our workers and=20
unions have exhibited unusual understanding and effort in=20
cooperating with management to achieve a cost structure and=20
product mix which might allow us to maintain a viable operation.=20
(2) Management also has been energetic and straightforward in its=20
approach to our textile problems.  In particular, Ken Chace=92s=20
efforts after the change in corporate control took place in 1965=20
generated capital from the textile division needed to finance the=20
acquisition and expansion of our profitable insurance operation. =20
(3) With hard work and some imagination regarding manufacturing=20
and marketing configurations, it seems reasonable that at least=20
modest profits in the textile division can be achieved in the=20
future.

<i>Insurance Underwriting</i>

     Our insurance operation continued to grow significantly in=20
1977.  It was early in 1967 that we made our entry into this=20
industry through the purchase of National Indemnity Company and=20
National Fire and Marine Insurance Company (sister companies) for=20
approximately $8.6 million.  In that year their premium volume=20
amounted to $22 million.  In 1977 our aggregate insurance premium=20
volume was $151 million.  No additional shares of Berkshire=20
Hathaway stock have been issued to achieve any of this growth.

     Rather, this almost 600% increase has been achieved through=20
large gains in National Indemnity=92s traditional liability areas=20
plus the starting of new companies (Cornhusker Casualty Company=20
in 1970, Lakeland Fire and Casualty Company in 1971, Texas United=20
Insurance Company in 1972, The Insurance Company of Iowa in 1973,=20
and Kansas Fire and Casualty Company in late 1977), the purchase=20
for cash of other insurance companies (Home and Automobile=20
Insurance Company in 1971, Kerkling Reinsurance Corporation, now=20
named Central Fire and Casualty Company, in 1976, and Cypress=20
Insurance Company at yearend 1977), and finally through the=20
marketing of additional products, most significantly reinsurance,=20
within the National Indemnity Company corporate structure.

     In aggregate, the insurance business has worked out very=20
well.  But it hasn=92t been a one-way street.  Some major mistakes=20
have been made during the decade, both in products and personnel. =20
We experienced significant problems from (1) a surety operation=20
initiated in 1969, (2) the 1973 expansion of Home and=20
Automobile=92s urban auto marketing into the Miami, Florida area,=20
(3) a still unresolved aviation =93fronting=94 arrangement, and (4)=20
our Worker=92s Compensation operation in California, which we=20
believe retains an interesting potential upon completion of a=20
reorganization now in progress.  It is comforting to be in a=20
business where some mistakes can be made and yet a quite=20
satisfactory overall performance can be achieved.  In a sense,=20
this is the opposite case from our textile business where even=20
very good management probably can average only modest results. =20
One of the lessons your management has learned - and,=20
unfortunately, sometimes re-learned - is the importance of being=20
in businesses where tailwinds prevail rather than headwinds.

     In 1977 the winds in insurance underwriting were squarely=20
behind us.  Very large rate increases were effected throughout=20
the industry in 1976 to offset the disastrous underwriting=20
results of 1974 and 1975.  But, because insurance policies=20
typically are written for one-year periods, with pricing mistakes=20
capable of correction only upon renewal, it was 1977 before the=20
full impact was felt upon earnings of those earlier rate=20
increases.

     The pendulum now is beginning to swing the other way.  We=20
estimate that costs involved in the insurance areas in which we=20
operate rise at close to 1% per month.  This is due to continuous=20
monetary inflation affecting the cost of repairing humans and=20
property, as well as =93social inflation=94, a broadening definition=20
by society and juries of what is covered by insurance policies. =20
Unless rates rise at a comparable 1% per month, underwriting=20
profits must shrink.  Recently the pace of rate increases has=20
slowed dramatically, and it is our expectation that underwriting=20
margins generally will be declining by the second half of the=20
year.

     We must again give credit to Phil Liesche, greatly assisted=20
by Roland Miller in Underwriting and Bill Lyons in Claims, for an=20
extraordinary underwriting achievement in National Indemnity=92s=20
traditional auto and general liability business during 1977. =20
Large volume gains have been accompanied by excellent=20
underwriting margins following contraction or withdrawal by many=20
competitors in the wake of the 1974-75 crisis period.  These=20
conditions will reverse before long.  In the meantime, National=20
Indemnity=92s underwriting profitability has increased dramatically=20
and, in addition, large sums have been made available for=20
investment.  As markets loosen and rates become inadequate, we=20
again will face the challenge of philosophically accepting=20
reduced volume.  Unusual managerial discipline will be required,=20
as it runs counter to normal institutional behavior to let the=20
other fellow take away business - even at foolish prices.

     Our reinsurance department, managed by George Young,=20
improved its underwriting performance during 1977.  Although the=20
combined ratio (see definition on page 12) of 107.1 was=20
unsatisfactory, its trend was downward throughout the year.  In=20
addition, reinsurance generates unusually high funds for=20
investment as a percentage of premium volume.

     At Home and Auto, John Seward continued to make progress on=20
all fronts.  John was a battlefield promotion several years ago=20
when Home and Auto=92s underwriting was awash in red ink and the=20
company faced possible extinction.  Under his management it=20
currently is sound, profitable, and growing.

     John Ringwalt=92s homestate operation now consists of five=20
companies, with Kansas Fire and Casualty Company becoming=20
operational late in 1977 under the direction of Floyd Taylor. =20
The homestate companies had net premium volume of $23 million, up=20
from $5.5 million just three years ago.  All four companies that=20
operated throughout the year achieved combined ratios below 100,=20
with Cornhusker Casualty Company, at 93.8, the leader.  In=20
addition to actively supervising the other four homestate=20
operations, John Ringwalt manages the operations of Cornhusker=20
which has recorded combined ratios below 100 in six of its seven=20
full years of existence and, from a standing start in 1970, has=20
grown to be one of the leading insurance companies operating in=20
Nebraska utilizing the conventional independent agency system. =20
Lakeland Fire and Casualty Company, managed by Jim Stodolka, was=20
the winner of the Chairman=92s Cup in 1977 for achieving the lowest=20
loss ratio among the homestate companies.  All in all, the=20
homestate operation continues to make excellent progress.

     The newest addition to our insurance group is Cypress=20
Insurance Company of South Pasadena, California.  This Worker=92s=20
Compensation insurer was purchased for cash in the final days of=20
1977 and, therefore, its approximate $12.5 million of volume for=20
that year was not included in our results.  Cypress and National=20
Indemnity=92s present California Worker=92s Compensation operation=20
will not be combined, but will operate independently utilizing=20
somewhat different marketing strategies.  Milt Thornton,=20
President of Cypress since 1968, runs a first-class operation for=20
policyholders, agents, employees and owners alike.  We look=20
forward to working with him.

     Insurance companies offer standardized policies which can be=20
copied by anyone.  Their only products are promises.  It is not=20
difficult to be licensed, and rates are an open book.  There are=20
no important advantages from trademarks, patents, location,=20
corporate longevity, raw material sources, etc., and very little=20
consumer differentiation to produce insulation from competition. =20
It is commonplace, in corporate annual reports, to stress the=20
difference that people make.  Sometimes this is true and=20
sometimes it isn=92t.  But there is no question that the nature of=20
the insurance business magnifies the effect which individual=20
managers have on company performance.  We are very fortunate to=20
have the group of managers that are associated with us.

<i>Insurance Investments</i>

     During the past two years insurance investments at cost=20
(excluding the investment in our affiliate, Blue Chip Stamps)=20
have grown from $134.6 million to $252.8 million.  Growth in=20
insurance reserves, produced by our large gain in premium volume,=20
plus retained earnings, have accounted for this increase in=20
marketable securities.  In turn, net investment income of the=20
Insurance Group has improved from $8.4 million pre-tax in 1975 to=20
$12.3 million pre-tax in 1977.

     In addition to this income from dividends and interest, we=20
realized capital gains of $6.9 million before tax, about one-
quarter from bonds and the balance from stocks.  Our unrealized=20
gain in stocks at yearend 1977 was approximately $74 million but=20
this figure, like any other figure of a single date (we had an=20
unrealized loss of $17 million at the end of 1974), should not be=20
taken too seriously.  Most of our large stock positions are going=20
to be held for many years and the scorecard on our investment=20
decisions will be provided by business results over that period,=20
and not by prices on any given day.  Just as it would be foolish=20
to focus unduly on short-term prospects when acquiring an entire=20
company, we think it equally unsound to become mesmerized by=20
prospective near term earnings or recent trends in earnings when=20
purchasing small pieces of a company; i.e., marketable common=20
stocks.

     A little digression illustrating this point may be=20
interesting.  Berkshire Fine Spinning Associates and Hathaway=20
Manufacturing were merged in 1955 to form Berkshire Hathaway Inc. =20
In 1948, on a pro forma combined basis, they had earnings after=20
tax of almost $18 million and employed 10,000 people at a dozen=20
large mills throughout New England.  In the business world of=20
that period they were an economic powerhouse.  For example, in=20
that same year earnings of IBM were $28 million (now $2.7=20
billion), Safeway Stores, $10 million, Minnesota Mining, $13=20
million, and Time, Inc., $9 million.  But, in the decade=20
following the 1955 merger aggregate sales of $595 million=20
produced an aggregate loss for Berkshire Hathaway of $10 million. =20
By 1964 the operation had been reduced to two mills and net worth=20
had shrunk to $22 million, from $53 million at the time of the=20
merger.  So much for single year snapshots as adequate portrayals=20
of a business.

