     We select our marketable equity securities in much the same=20
way we would evaluate a business for acquisition in its entirety. =20
We want the business to be (1) one that we can understand, (2)=20
with favorable long-term prospects, (3) operated by honest and=20
competent people, and (4) available at a very attractive price. =20
We ordinarily make no attempt to buy equities for anticipated=20
favorable stock price behavior in the short term.  In fact, if=20
their business experience continues to satisfy us, we welcome=20
lower market prices of stocks we own as an opportunity to acquire=20
even more of a good thing at a better price.

     Our experience has been that pro-rata portions of truly=20
outstanding businesses sometimes sell in the securities markets=20
at very large discounts from the prices they would command in=20
negotiated transactions involving entire companies. =20
Consequently, bargains in business ownership, which simply are=20
not available directly through corporate acquisition, can be=20
obtained indirectly through stock ownership.  When prices are=20
appropriate, we are willing to take very large positions in=20
selected companies, not with any intention of taking control and=20
not foreseeing sell-out or merger, but with the expectation that=20
excellent business results by corporations will translate over=20
the long term into correspondingly excellent market value and=20
dividend results for owners, minority as well as majority.

     Such investments initially may have negligible impact on our=20
operating earnings.  For example, we invested $10.9 million in=20
Capital Cities Communications during 1977.  Earnings attributable=20
to the shares we purchased totaled about $1.3 million last year. =20
But only the cash dividend, which currently provides $40,000=20
annually, is reflected in our operating earnings figure.

     Capital Cities possesses both extraordinary properties and=20
extraordinary management.  And these management skills extend=20
equally to operations and employment of corporate capital.  To=20
purchase, directly, properties such as Capital Cities owns would=20
cost in the area of twice our cost of purchase via the stock=20
market, and direct ownership would offer no important advantages=20
to us.  While control would give us the opportunity - and the=20
responsibility - to manage operations and corporate resources, we=20
would not be able to provide management in either of those=20
respects equal to that now in place.  In effect, we can obtain a=20
better management result through non-control than control.  This=20
is an unorthodox view, but one we believe to be sound.

Banking

     In 1977 the Illinois National Bank continued to achieve a=20
rate of earnings on assets about three times that of most large=20
banks.  As usual, this record was achieved while the bank paid=20
maximum rates to savers and maintained an asset position=20
combining low risk and exceptional liquidity.  Gene Abegg formed=20
the bank in 1931 with $250,000.  In its first full year of=20
operation, earnings amounted to $8,782.  Since that time, no new=20
capital has been contributed to the bank; on the contrary, since=20
our purchase in 1969, dividends of $20 million have been paid. =20
Earnings in 1977 amounted to $3.6 million, more than achieved by=20
many banks two or three times its size.

     Late last year Gene, now 80 and still running a banking=20
operation without peer, asked that a successor be brought in. =20
Accordingly, Peter Jeffrey, formerly President and Chief=20
Executive Officer of American National Bank of Omaha, has joined=20
the Illinois National Bank effective March 1st as President and=20
Chief Executive Officer.

     Gene continues in good health as Chairman.  We expect a=20
continued successful operation at Rockford=92s leading bank.

Blue Chip Stamps

     We again increased our equity interest in Blue Chip Stamps,=20
and owned approximately 36 1/2% at the end of 1977.  Blue Chip=20
had a fine year, earning approximately $12.9 million from=20
operations and, in addition, had realized securities gains of=20
$4.1 million.

     Both Wesco Financial Corp., an 80% owned subsidiary of Blue=20
Chip Stamps, managed by Louis Vincenti, and See=92s Candies, a 99%=20
owned subsidiary, managed by Chuck Huggins, made good progress in=20
1977.  Since See=92s was purchased by Blue Chip Stamps at the=20
beginning of 1972, pre-tax operating earnings have grown from=20
$4.2 million to $12.6 million with little additional capital=20
investment.  See=92s achieved this record while operating in an=20
industry experiencing practically no unit growth.  Shareholders=20
of Berkshire Hathaway Inc. may obtain the annual report of Blue=20
Chip Stamps by requesting it from Mr. Robert H. Bird, Blue Chip=20
Stamps, 5801 South Eastern Avenue, Los Angeles, California 90040.


                                    Warren E. Buffett, Chairman

March 14,1978

